<!--
  Navigate to a pose, replanning twice a second, with recovery.

  default_nav2_bt.xml plans once and has no way out when the robot is wedged. This tree is the
  stock Nav2 "navigate to pose with replanning and recovery" tree with these changes:

    - the Spin recovery is removed. The robot's tail reaches 0.9 m behind the drive axle and
      sweeps a 1.8 m circle when it spins, so spinning in a 1.5 m passage hits things.

    - ITERATION 1: the cost map clears are switched back on, and placed LAST.
      Every ClearEntireCostmap in this tree was commented out when the four real bags were
      recorded, which left the round robin as BackUp, Wait, BackUp, Wait. The bags show why that
      could never work. Both cost maps had ray-trace clearing disabled, so the walls grew inward
      all run until no footprint-feasible route to the goal was left and the PLANNER failed -
      /plan stops one tree tick before the recovery fires, in all three failed runs. Nothing in
      this tree removed a stale mark, so the one thing that would have freed the robot was the
      one thing it could not do. Both BackUps then aborted after 0.07-0.10 m of the 0.6 and 0.8 m
      they were asked for, because the tail was already inside a band of cells that the cost map
      wrongly believed were lethal.
      Clearing is a last resort, so it is the last pair of entries in the round robin: the robot
      tries to reverse and look again twice before it is allowed to forget anything, and it
      forgets the local map on its own before it forgets both. The real fix for the stale marks
      is clearing: True in Course2027.yaml; these entries are the safety net for when a mark is
      stale for some other reason.

    - ITERATION 1: the round robin ends with a longer Wait after the full clear, so the LiDAR,
      the lane points and the route corridor are all marked again before the next attempt, and
      number_of_retries is 8 rather than 6 so that there are attempts left after the clears
      instead of the clears being the last thing that ever happens.

    - ITERATION 1: replanning raised from 1 Hz to 2 Hz. The cost maps now change meaningfully
      between ticks (they clear as well as mark), and at 0.5 m/s a 1 Hz plan was up to 1.1 s and
      half a metre stale by the time the controller used it; the probe of the stopped runs found
      the controller working from a 1.12 s old plan. Planning measured under 0.1 s in the bags,
      and the global cost map is now 11x smaller, so 2 Hz is affordable.

    - ITERATION 3: the two error-code gates are spelled out with AreErrorCodesPresent instead
      of WouldAControllerRecoveryHelp / WouldAPlannerRecoveryHelp. Those two nodes carry fixed
      code lists that leave out ComputePathToPose 205 START_OCCUPIED, 206 GOAL_OCCUPIED and
      FollowPath 107 CONTROLLER_TIMED_OUT. Run 8 of iteration 3 failed on 205 and the gate
      refused it, so the round robin below never ran at all - see the note on the gate itself.

  A robot facing a genuinely closed passage will forget the dead end here and drive at it again.
  The lap manager stops such a lap (no ground gained along the route for 75 s); indoors, the
  tester is the lap manager.

  It needs the behavior server (launch with use_recoveries:=true).
-->
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="8" name="NavigateRecovery">
      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
        <ControllerSelector selected_controller="{selected_controller}" default_controller="FollowPath" topic_name="controller_selector"/>
        <PlannerSelector selected_planner="{selected_planner}" default_planner="GridBased" topic_name="planner_selector"/>
        <RateController hz="2.0">
          <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="{selected_planner}" error_code_id="{compute_path_error_code}"/>
        </RateController>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="FollowPath">
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="{selected_controller}" error_code_id="{follow_path_error_code}"/>
          <Sequence>
            <!-- ITERATION 3: was WouldAControllerRecoveryHelp, which hard-codes
                 {100 UNKNOWN, 104 PATIENCE_EXCEEDED, 105 FAILED_TO_MAKE_PROGRESS,
                 106 NO_VALID_CONTROL} and so does not include 107 CONTROLLER_TIMED_OUT.
                 Spelling the list out adds 107 and makes it visible what is covered.
                 101 INVALID_CONTROLLER, 102 TF_ERROR and 103 INVALID_PATH are left out on
                 purpose: those are configuration faults that no amount of retrying fixes. -->
            <AreErrorCodesPresent error_code="{follow_path_error_code}"
                                  error_codes_to_check="100;104;105;106;107"/>
            <!-- Deliberately empty of any clear: a single bad control cycle is not a reason to
                 throw away what the cost maps know. controller_server.failure_tolerance absorbs
                 those, and anything worse drops through to the round robin below. -->
          </Sequence>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </PipelineSequence>
      <Sequence>
        <!-- ITERATION 3: THIS GATE IS WHY RUN 8 DIED WITH THE RECOVERY UNUSED.
             At t=13.62 the planner logged
               GridBasedplugin failed to plan from (2.14, 1.69) to (1.55, 6.42): "Start occupied"
             which is ComputePathToPose error 205, START_OCCUPIED. WouldAPlannerRecoveryHelp
             only matches {200 UNKNOWN, 207 TIMEOUT, 208 NO_VALID_PATH}, so it returned FAILURE,
             the controller condition returned FAILURE too, this Fallback failed, and the whole
             round robin below never ticked once: no BackUp, no Wait, no clear. The run aborted
             3.1 m in. Backing up is exactly the right answer to START_OCCUPIED, and the robot
             was never allowed to try it.
             Listing the codes explicitly adds 205 START_OCCUPIED and 206 GOAL_OCCUPIED, and
             keeps out the codes a recovery genuinely cannot help with: 201 INVALID_PLANNER,
             202 TF_ERROR, 203 START_OUTSIDE_MAP, 204 GOAL_OUTSIDE_MAP. -->
        <Fallback>
          <AreErrorCodesPresent error_code="{follow_path_error_code}"
                                error_codes_to_check="100;104;105;106;107"/>
          <AreErrorCodesPresent error_code="{compute_path_error_code}"
                                error_codes_to_check="200;205;206;207;208"/>
        </Fallback>
        <ReactiveFallback name="RecoveryFallback">
          <GoalUpdated/>
          <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
            <!-- ITERATION 4: THE GATE ABOVE NOW WORKS AND EVERY RUNG BELOW IT DID NOT.
                 Runs 3, 4 and 10 all entered this round robin. Between them they made
                 EIGHT BackUp attempts and all eight aborted with error 714,
                   behavior_server: "Collision Ahead - Exiting DriveOnHeading"
                 - four of them in under 20 ms, before the robot moved at all. The round
                 robin therefore reached ClearEntireCostmap in 5.4 s (run 4), 5.1 s
                 (run 3) and 8.1 s (run 10), and runs 4 and 10 went on to clear BOTH
                 maps. Clearing is supposed to be the last resort; it was in practice the
                 first rung that did anything.

                 Why BackUp aborts, from nav2_behaviors DriveOnHeading::isCollisionFree:
                 it simulates the reverse forward by simulate_ahead_time (1.0 s here) at
                 backup_speed, stopping early once it has covered the requested distance,
                 and vetoes the WHOLE manoeuvre if the footprint at any simulated pose
                 covers a cell of cost 254. At backup_speed 0.25 that projection was
                 0.25 m. This robot's tail reaches 0.86 m behind base_link, and these
                 aborts all happened where the measured footprint clearance was 2.5-5 cm,
                 so 0.25 m of straight reverse always put the tail outline on a lethal
                 cell.

                 Two changes, both aimed at letting a reverse actually start:
                   * backup_speed 0.25 -> 0.10, so the veto looks 0.10 m ahead instead of
                     0.25 m. This does NOT remove the protection: onCycleUpdate re-runs
                     the same check every cycle at 10 Hz, so the reverse still stops
                     before contact - it just is not refused outright before it begins.
                   * the first reverse is shortened to 0.30 m, because the projection is
                     also capped by the distance still to run, so a shorter request is
                     checked over a shorter span.
                 A reverse is now slower (0.30 m takes 3 s) and that is accepted: a
                 recovery that takes three seconds and works beats one that takes ten
                 milliseconds and fails.

              1. Stand still FIRST. This used to be step 2. Nothing moves, so it can never
                 be refused, and it is the one rung that was always going to run: STVL's
                 in-frustum decay clears an unseen voxel in about 1 s at
                 decay_acceleration 5.0 (measured in iteration 3), so two seconds is
                 enough to drop a transient mark before anything is asked to reverse into
                 it. If the blockage was transient, the replan after this rung succeeds
                 and nothing else in the round robin is ever reached. -->
            <Wait wait_duration="2.0"/>
            <!-- 2. Back off a little and look again. 0.30 m at 0.10 m/s. -->
            <BackUp backup_dist="0.30" backup_speed="0.10" error_code_id="{backup_error_code}"/>
            <!-- 3. Another full sweep's worth of standing still. -->
            <Wait wait_duration="2.0"/>
            <!-- 4. Back off further, same speed so the same 0.10 m veto window applies. -->
            <BackUp backup_dist="0.50" backup_speed="0.10" error_code_id="{backup_error_code}"/>
            <Wait wait_duration="2.0"/>
            <!-- 5. LAST RESORT, step one: forget the local map only. The global map keeps the
                    route, so the robot cannot wander off it while the controller re-reads the
                    space immediately around the robot. -->
            <Sequence name="ClearLocalOnly">
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-LastResort" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
              <Wait wait_duration="2.0"/>
            </Sequence>
            <!-- 6. LAST RESORT, step two: forget both maps and rebuild from the sensors. The wall
                    layer is not clearable, so the virtual wall at the start line survives this. -->
            <Sequence name="ClearEverything">
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Full" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Full" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
              <Wait wait_duration="3.0"/>
            </Sequence>
          </RoundRobin>
        </ReactiveFallback>
      </Sequence>
    </RecoveryNode>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
